{\"id\":\"fs_119\",\"fact\":\"Lewis Carroll (Charles Dodgson) embedded sophisticated mathematics into Alice in Wonderland: base-numbering arithmetic jokes (changing bases from 18 to 24+), non-Euclidean geometry critiques via the Mad Hatter's stopped time, and 'property without carrier' abstraction via the Cheshire Cat's disembodied smile — reflecting his lifelong opposition to modern abstract mathematics at Oxford Senior Common Room in the 1860s. He published Euclid and His Modern Rivals in 1879 defending classical geometry against Riemann's innovations.\",\"source\":\"https://storica.club (HN 29pts, 2026-05-19)\",\"date\":\"2026-05-19\",\"tags\":\"persistent,literature,mathematics,history,culture,hidden-depth\",\"confidence\":0.92}
